## The temple under the market

Esna is a working town on the Nile, all traffic, fabric shops and fruit stalls. In the middle of it, behind a railing, the ground simply drops away. Nine metres down stands the Temple of Khnum, or what survives of it: one great hall, roofed and whole, which the town slowly rose around over two thousand years until the temple sat in a pit of its own history.

You walk down the steps, out of the noise, and into the shade of twenty-four columns.

## A sky that came back

For centuries the ceiling was black. Smoke, dust and grime had settled over every surface, and visitors saw grey stone and the shapes of figures. Then an Egyptian–German restoration team began to clean it, square metre by square metre, and the original paint came back: deep blues, ochres, greens, constellations, the waxing and waning moon, the gods of the four winds, and rows of painted birds with their wings spread across the central ceiling. The work on the ceiling was completed in 2023.

It means that Esna, a temple many travellers drive past, now holds colour that looks as if the painters had only just put down their brushes.

> You walk out of a market, down a flight of steps, and look up into a sky someone painted two thousand years ago.

## Khnum, the potter god

The temple belongs to Khnum, the ram-headed creator who, the Egyptians said, shaped every human being on his potter's wheel. What you see dates from the Roman period, when emperors had themselves carved as pharaohs making offerings to an Egyptian god. On the Original day, your concierge takes you there and leaves you to look up for as long as you like; on the Signature day, a licensed Egyptologist can join you to read those walls.

## Left to us

An hour's drive south of Luxor, along the river road through cane fields and palms. When to go, how to arrive, what to settle at the gate: your concierge takes care of all of it, with local knowledge and our craft. It makes a calm, colourful morning before Luxor's West Bank, or the first stop on the Temple Road, our private day south to Aswan by way of Edfu and Kom Ombo.
